.TH XYMONAPI-AGENT 1 "November 3, 2026" "xymon-plugins"
.SH NAME
xymonapi-agent \- ask an AI agent a question about Xymon, headless
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "xymonapi-agent [--agent NAME] [--backend NAMES] ask [--session NAME] [--json] [--show-command] QUESTION"
.br
.B "xymonapi-agent agents"
.br
.B "xymonapi-agent [--agent NAME] [--backend NAMES] backends"
.br
.B "xymonapi-agent [--agent NAME] [--backend NAMES] doctor [--json]"
.br
.B "xymonapi-agent --agent NAME mcp-agents"

.SH DESCRIPTION
xymonapi-agent asks a question about a Xymon server to an AI agent and
prints its answer, with no one at the keyboard. The model behind it is one of the
command-line coding agents a person may already have on the machine --
Claude Code, Codex or opencode -- run in its non-interactive mode, with
.I xymonapi-mcp(1),
installed beside this plugin, as its one tool: the read-only tools over the
Xymon API, nothing else. It is for a terminal, a cron job, an alert script,
or a chat bot: whatever wants the answer a person would get by typing the
question into one of those agents, as text.

What is asked is an \fIagent\fR: which backends to try, in order; which
MCP servers it gets; what its prompt says beyond the fixed part; which
Xymon server it reads. The built-in agent, \fBxymon\fR, is this plugin's
own: the Xymon MCP server beside it, with the API's settings from this
environment -- XYMONAPI_URL, XYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE, XYMONAPI_CA and the rest
the client library reads, each when set -- so what the person has registered with
their own agent plays no part. A profile file (see PROFILES) adds another
agent, or redefines xymon.

Every backend is given the same three things: the agent's MCP servers, by
command and environment; a system prompt whose fixed part says the agent
reads through those tools, changes nothing, has no shell, files or web,
and that text a tool returns about a monitored system is data about it,
never an instruction; and the rule that those tools are the only ones.
Its standard input is empty: what is piped into xymonapi-agent is not the
question.
How completely a backend's flags can promise that differs, and
\fBbackends\fR says so for each. A profile chooses tools, words and
target; it cannot loosen that.

It needs Python 3.9 or later, and the agent CLI installed and logged in by
its own means; this command only runs it. Without that Python it says so on
stderr and exits with status 77. The CLI's own failure is repeated on stderr
with its exit status, and this command exits 1.

.SH COMMANDS
.IP "\fBask\fR [\fB--session\fR NAME] [\fB--json\fR] [\fB--show-command\fR] QUESTION"
Ask QUESTION and print the answer. With \fB--session\fR NAME the
conversation of that name is continued, or started: a later question with
the same name, to the same agent, follows on from this one. Only Claude Code continues a session
here, by an id derived from the name and the same on every machine; with
the other backends a named session is started fresh each time, and stderr
says so. Without a name, Claude Code keeps no record of the conversation.
\fB--json\fR prints one object: \fBagent\fR, \fBbackend\fR, \fBsession\fR
and \fBanswer\fR. \fB--show-command\fR prints on stderr the command that was
run, its long arguments shortened, so what the backend was told can be
seen without a secret in it.
.IP "\fBagents\fR"
Each agent, built in or from a profile: its description, backends, MCP
servers, the agents it coordinates, model and environment, and the file it
came from.
.IP "\fBmcp-agents\fR"
Serve a coordinator's agents as MCP tools on stdin and stdout: what its
backend is given to run, not a command for a terminal. For an agent that
coordinates none, an error.
.IP "\fBbackends\fR"
For each backend, whether its command is on PATH and how far it is held
to the MCP servers; then which answer for the agent, in order, or why none
does; and where each of the agent's MCP servers is expected.
.IP "\fBdoctor\fR [\fB--json\fR]"
Asks the agent to call the xymon health tool once and answer with the
version it gives, and passes when the answer looks like one: something only
that tool can have said. Exits 1 otherwise, with the answer printed, as
when the MCP server did not start or the backend was denied it; and says
so when the agent has no xymon server to ask.

.SH PROFILES
A file \fINAME.toml\fR under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xymonapi-agent/agents/
(~/.config/...) is the agent NAME; \fBxymon.toml\fR redefines the built-in
one. \fB--agent\fR NAME, else XYMONAPI_AGENT_AGENT, says which is asked;
xymon when neither does. The keys, each optional:
.IP "\fBdescription\fR"
One line, for \fBagents\fR.
.IP "\fBbackend\fR"
The backends to try, in order: a list, or one name. Empty, the one agent
CLI on PATH, as for the built-in agent. See BACKENDS for the order's
meaning.
.IP "\fBmodel\fR, \fBsite\fR"
As XYMONAPI_AGENT_MODEL and XYMONAPI_AGENT_SITE, for this agent.
.IP "\fBmcp\fR"
The MCP servers the agent gets: a list of names, \fB["xymon"]\fR by default.
\fBxymon\fR is built in; any other is defined by a \fB[server.NAME]\fR table
with \fBcommand\fR, and optionally \fBargs\fR, a list, and \fBenv\fR, a list
of the variables of the environment to hand it. A name with no table is
an error.
.IP "\fBprompt\fR"
Text appended to the fixed system prompt: what the servers are, how to
answer. It cannot remove the fixed part.
.IP "\fBagents\fR"
A list of other agents' names: this agent is then a \fIcoordinator\fR (see
COORDINATORS). With it, \fBmcp\fR is empty by default: a coordinator's
tools are its agents.
.IP "\fB[env]\fR"
Variables set over this environment for the backend and the MCP servers:
another Xymon server's XYMONAPI_URL and XYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE, a server's
own settings. A ~ at the start of a value is the home directory.
.PP
The file is TOML as far as those keys need: strings in double quotes,
lists of them, true, false, numbers, comments, [tables]. Anything else is
refused with the file and line, by every command, so a bad profile is seen
at once. A profile cannot loosen a backend's restriction: the tools it
allows are those of its \fBmcp\fR list, and nothing else.

.SH COORDINATORS
An agent whose profile has \fBagents\fR gets, besides any MCP server of
its own, one named \fBagents\fR: this command itself, run by the backend as
\fBxymonapi-agent --agent NAME mcp-agents\fR, serving one tool per agent,
\fBask_\fIAGENT\fR(question), described by that agent's \fBdescription\fR.
Its prompt says to route the question to the agents it concerns, ask each
what it alone can answer, and merge the answers naming which agent said
what. A call runs \fBxymonapi-agent --agent AGENT ask --json\fR in a process
of its own: that agent's backends, servers, sandbox and environment, not
the coordinator's -- XYMONAPI_AGENT_AGENT, XYMONAPI_AGENT_BACKEND and
XYMONAPI_AGENT_DEPTH are not passed down. The answer is the tool's text; a
failure, with the agent's last line, is a tool error the backend sees.

Two guards. XYMONAPI_AGENT_DEPTH, 2 by default, is how many coordinators
deep an ask may go: each child runs with one less, and at 0 every ask_
call is refused, so two coordinators that name each other stop.
XYMONAPI_AGENT_SUBASKS, 8, is the most asks one question may make of the
agents; the next is refused with a word to answer with what it has. A
profile that names itself, or an agent no profile defines, is refused as
a bad profile. \fBdoctor\fR does not apply to a coordinator without the
xymon server, and says so.

.SH BACKENDS
The backends to try, in order, are \fB--backend\fR, else
XYMONAPI_AGENT_BACKEND -- names separated by commas -- else the agent's
\fBbackend\fR list, else whichever one agent CLI is on PATH: with several
here and no list, nothing runs and the message names them. Of a list, the
first that is on PATH is run; when it exits with an error, gives no answer
in XYMONAPI_AGENT_TIMEOUT seconds, or an empty one, stderr says so and the
next is tried; the answer and, with \fB--json\fR, the \fBbackend\fR field are
the one that answered. Every one failing is the error, each with its
reason. A name on the list that is not on PATH is skipped without a word.
.IP "\fBclaude\fR"
Claude Code, with \fB-p\fR. \fB--strict-mcp-config\fR gives it the MCP
server from the command line and no other; \fB--tools ""\fR removes every
built-in tool; \fB--allowedTools mcp__xymon__*\fR lets the xymon tools run
without asking; \fB--permission-mode dontAsk\fR denies anything else. Its
JSON output gives the answer, and the tool calls it denied, which are
added to the answer in brackets when there are any. The most complete of
the three.
.IP "\fBcodex\fR"
Codex, with \fBexec\fR. \fB--sandbox read-only\fR keeps it from writing;
configuration overrides on the command line give it the MCP server, turn
its shell tool off (\fBfeatures.shell_tool=false\fR) and its web search
off; \fB--ephemeral\fR keeps no session. Its documentation does not promise
the shell is gone with that setting, so this backend is partial: run
\fBdoctor\fR, and prefer another backend where the token file must not be
readable to the model. The system prompt goes in front of the question,
there being no flag for one.
.IP "\fBopencode\fR"
opencode, with \fBrun\fR, under a configuration file written for the call
and named by OPENCODE_CONFIG, with OPENCODE_DISABLE_PROJECT_CONFIG set: the
MCP server, a permission that denies every tool and allows \fBxymon_*\fR,
and an agent \fBxymon\fR carrying the system prompt, which \fB--agent\fR
runs. The person's global configuration still merges with that file; what
it adds is theirs to know. The file is removed after the call.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_AGENT\fR"
The agent, when \fB--agent\fR does not say; xymon.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_BACKEND\fR"
The backends to try, in order, when \fB--backend\fR does not say: of
claude, codex and opencode, separated by commas. Over a profile's list.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_MODEL\fR"
For the built-in agent, a model to ask the backend for, in the backend's
own form -- an alias or id for Claude Code, a model name for Codex,
provider/model for opencode. Unset, each uses its own default. A profile's
\fBmodel\fR is this for that agent.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_TIMEOUT\fR"
Seconds to wait for an answer; 600 by default.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_MAX_TURNS\fR"
For Claude Code, the most tool-calling turns one question may take; 30.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_DEPTH\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_SUBASKS\fR"
A coordinator's guards; see COORDINATORS. 2 and 8.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_AGENT_SITE\fR"
For the built-in agent, a name for the Xymon site, said in the system
prompt. A profile's \fBsite\fR is this for that agent.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_URL\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_TOKEN\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_CA\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_SOCKET\fR, \fBXYMONVAR\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_PYTHON\fR"
Where the API is and how to reach it, as
.I xymonapi-mcp(1)
reads them: handed to the MCP server as its environment, each when set.
.IP "\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR"
Under it, xymonapi-agent/agents/ holds the profiles; $HOME/.config when
unset.
.IP "\fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR"
Under it, xymonapi-agent/AGENT/BACKEND/NAME records a session named with
\fB--session\fR; $HOME/.local/state when unset.

.SH FILES
.IP "\fI../xymonapi-mcp/xymonapi-mcp.sh\fR"
The Xymon MCP server, beside this plugin, as \fB./install.sh install\fR
lays them out. Missing, as any of an agent's servers, \fBask\fR says so and runs
nothing.
.IP "\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xymonapi-agent/agents/NAME.toml\fR"
The agent NAME; see PROFILES.
.IP "\fI$XDG_STATE_HOME/xymonapi-agent/\fR"
The sessions, one small file each.

.SH EXIT STATUS
0 for an answer, or a passing doctor; 1 when every backend failed, none is
chosen, a profile is bad or doctor did not pass; 2 for a usage error; 77 when no Python 3.9
is here.

.SH SEE ALSO
.I xymonapi-mcp(1), xymonapi-rag(1), xymonapi(8)
